  "question": {
    "text": "Ejection of the photoelectron from metal in the photoelectric effect experiment can be stopped by applying $0.5 \\mathrm{~V}$ when the radiation of $250 \\mathrm{~nm}$ is used. The work function of the metal is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$4 \\mathrm{~eV}$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$5.5 \\mathrm{~eV}$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$4.5 \\mathrm{~eV}$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$5 \\mathrm{~eV}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\lambda=250 \\mathrm{~nm}=2500 Å$\n$\\mathrm{E}=\\frac{\\mathrm{hc}}{\\lambda}=\\frac{12400}{2500}=4.96 \\mathrm{~eV}$\n$\\mathrm{KE}=$ stopping potential $=0.5 \\mathrm{~eV}$\n$\\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{W}_0+\\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{E}$.\n$4.96=\\mathrm{W}_0+0.5$\n$\\mathrm{~W}_0=4.46 \\approx 4.5 \\mathrm{~eV}$"
  }
}